# 🚀 Multi-Leader Replication (Multi-Primary Replication)

> **Goal:** Multiple databases can accept **WRITE** requests simultaneously. This removes the single write bottleneck of Single Leader Replication and is useful for **globally distributed applications**.

---

# 📌 What is Multi-Leader Replication?

In **Single Leader Replication**

```
Only ONE Leader

↓

All Writes

↓

One Database
```

In **Multi-Leader Replication**

```
Multiple Leaders

↓

All can accept Writes
```

Every leader is both

- Writer ✍️
- Replica 📦

for the other leaders.

---

## Architecture

```
             Users

      🌍 USA      🌍 Europe      🌍 India
         |             |             |
         v             v             v

     +---------+   +---------+   +---------+
     |Leader A |<->|Leader B |<->|Leader C |
     +---------+<->+---------+<->+---------+

        (Every Leader talks to every other Leader)
```

Each leader accepts local writes and later synchronizes them with the others.

---

# 🤔 Why do we need Multi-Leader Replication?

Single Leader has one major problem.

```
All Writes

↓

One Leader

↓

Bottleneck 😭
```

If millions of users write simultaneously,

Leader becomes overloaded.

Multi-Leader solves this.

---

# 🎯 Benefits of Multi-Leader Replication

## 1️⃣ Higher Write Throughput

Since every leader accepts writes,

load gets distributed.

Without Multi-Leader

```
1 Leader

↓

10000 Writes/sec
```

With Multi-Leader

```
Leader A

3000 Writes/sec

Leader B

3500 Writes/sec

Leader C

3500 Writes/sec
```

Load divided.

---

## 2️⃣ Lower Geographic Latency

Imagine users from different countries.

Without Multi-Leader

```
India User

↓

USA Leader
```

Every write crosses continents.

Latency becomes high.

---

With Multi-Leader

```
India User

↓

India Leader

↓

Replicate to USA

↓

Replicate to Europe
```

Local writes become much faster.

---

## Real Life Example 🌍

Google Docs

People editing

```
India

USA

Germany
```

Everyone writes to the nearest server.

Later,

servers synchronize.

