# 🏗️ Replication Topologies

Topology means

> **How leaders communicate with each other.**

There are mainly **3 topologies.**

```
Replication Topologies

│

├── Circle

├── Star

└── All-to-All
```

---

# 🔄 1. Circle Topology

Every node sends data to only one neighbour.

```
      Leader A
      /      \
     /        \
Leader D ---- Leader B
      \      /
       \    /
      Leader C
```

Or conceptually

```
A → B → C → D → A
```

Replication happens in a circle.

---

## Example

```
Write at A

↓

Sent to B

↓

Sent to C

↓

Sent to D

↓

Back to A
```

---

## Problem ❌

Suppose

```
Leader B crashes.
```

```
A → ❌ → C → D
```

Replication stops.

Nodes after B won't receive updates.

---

## Advantages

✅ Simple

✅ Easy implementation

---

## Disadvantages

❌ Single node failure affects the whole chain

❌ Slow propagation

❌ Poor fault tolerance

---

# ⭐ 2. Star Topology

One central node connects everyone.

```
            Leader B

               |

Leader A ---- Hub ---- Leader C

               |

            Leader D
```

All communication goes through one central node.

---

## Example

```
Leader A

↓

Hub

↓

Leader C
```

Every message passes through the hub.

---

## Problem ❌

If Hub crashes

```
Leader A

×

Hub ❌

×

Leader C
```

Nobody can communicate.

Whole replication stops.

---

## Advantages

✅ Easy routing

✅ Centralized management

---

## Disadvantages

❌ Single Point of Failure

---

# 🌐 3. All-to-All Topology (Most Common)

Every leader connects with every other leader.

```
        A -------- B

        |\        /|

        | \      / |

        |  \    /  |

        |   \  /   |

        |    \/    |

        |    /\    |

        |   /  \   |

        |  /    \  |

        | /      \ |

        |/        \|

        D -------- C
```

Every leader directly exchanges updates.

---

## Example

Leader A writes.

```
A

↓

B

↓

C

↓

D
```

Actually

```
A sends directly to

B

C

D
```

No intermediate node required.

---

## Advantages

✅ Fast replication

✅ High durability

✅ No central bottleneck

✅ Failure tolerant

---

## Disadvantages

⚠️ Ordering becomes difficult.

⚠️ Causality problems.

⚠️ Conflict handling becomes harder.

---

# 🤯 Why does All-to-All create ordering problems?

Suppose

```
Leader A

Write 1
```

At the same time

```
Leader B

Write 2
```

Leader C may receive

```
Write 2

↓

Write 1
```

Leader D may receive

```
Write 1

↓

Write 2
```

Different order.

Different databases.

---

## Visualization

```
Leader A

Write X

↓

Leader C

(Received Late)

-------------------

Leader B

Write Y

↓

Leader C

(Received First)
```

Leader C thinks

```
Y happened before X
```

Reality

Maybe opposite.

---

# 📦 Replication Log Management

Every leader maintains a **Replication Log**.

It stores

- Which writes happened
- Which nodes have already received them

---

## Why?

Imagine

Leader A sends

```
Write #105
```

Leader B forwards it back.

Leader A receives the same write again.

Without tracking

```
Apply Again ❌

Duplicate Data
```

---

## Solution

Maintain metadata.

```
Write #105

Seen By

Leader A ✅

Leader B ✅

Leader C ❌
```

Now Leader A knows

"I've already processed this."

Ignore duplicate.

---

## Real Life Example

WhatsApp Blue Tick.

```
Message Seen

↓

Don't notify again.
```

Same idea.

---

# ⚠️ Write Conflicts

Most important interview topic.

Conflict means

Two leaders modify the same data simultaneously.

---

## Example

Suppose

User Name

```
John
```

Leader A

```
John

↓

Johnny
```

Leader B

```
John

↓

Jonathan
```

Now

Replication starts.

Question

Which name is correct?

```
Johnny?

Jonathan?
```

No obvious answer.

---

# Visualization

```
Leader A

Name = Johnny

------------

Leader B

Name = Jonathan
```

Conflict.

---

# Why is this difficult?

Both writes are valid.

Both happened independently.

Database cannot magically know

which one user actually wanted.

---

# 🛡️ Conflict Avoidance

Simplest solution

Assign one leader for one key.

Example

```
User 1-1000

↓

Leader A

----------------

User 1001-2000

↓

Leader B
```

Each key has one owner.

No conflicts.

---

## This is called

```
Sharding

or

Partitioning
```

---

## Advantage

Almost zero write conflicts.

---

## Disadvantage

Write throughput becomes limited.

Because

```
User 500

Always

↓

Leader A
```

Even if other leaders are idle.

---

# ⚔️ Conflict Resolution

Eventually,

conflicts will happen.

Most common strategy

```
Last Write Wins (LWW)
```

---

# ⏰ Last Write Wins (LWW)

Idea

Keep the newest write.

Example

```
10:00

John

↓

10:05

Johnny
```

Winner

```
Johnny
```

Older write discarded.

---

## Sounds Easy?

Actually...

Not at all.

Because timestamps themselves are unreliable.

---

# ⚠️ Problem 1 : Clock Skew

Every computer has its own hardware clock.

No two clocks are perfectly identical.

```
Server A

10:00:05

------------

Server B

09:59:58
```

Difference exists.

This is called

> **Clock Skew**

---

## Why?

Computers use

```
Quartz Crystal Oscillator
```

Small manufacturing differences

Temperature

Hardware aging

cause clocks to drift.

Even a few milliseconds matter in distributed systems.

---

## Real Life Analogy

Imagine

Three wall clocks.

```
Clock A

10:00

Clock B

10:01

Clock C

09:59
```

Which one is correct?

Nobody knows.

Same happens with servers.

---

# ⚠️ Problem 2 : NTP (Network Time Protocol)

To fix clock differences,

servers synchronize using NTP.

```
Server

↓

Internet

↓

Time Server
```

---

## But...

Network itself has delay.

```
Time Server

↓

Packet Delay

↓

Server
```

Delay varies every request.

Sometimes

```
10 ms

Sometimes

300 ms
```

Clock suddenly jumps.

---

## Example

Server thinks

```
10:00:10
```

NTP corrects it to

```
09:59:55
```

Time moved backward.

Now

Older writes may look newer.

---

## Why is this dangerous?

Imagine

```
Write A

10:01

------------

Clock Corrected

------------

Write B

09:59
```

Database believes

```
Write A is newer.
```

Reality

Write B actually happened later.

Wrong winner.

Possible data loss.

---

# 💥 Biggest Limitation of LWW

LWW chooses

one winner

and discards the other write.

```
Leader A

Johnny

------------

Leader B

Jonathan

------------

Winner

Johnny

------------

Jonathan Lost Forever ❌
```

Information disappears.

---

# 🧠 Is there a perfect solution?

Unfortunately...

❌ No.

There is **no universal conflict resolution strategy** that works perfectly for every application.

Different systems use different techniques based on their business requirements.

Examples include

- Last Write Wins (LWW)
- Application-level merge logic
- Version Vectors / Vector Clocks
- CRDTs (Conflict-Free Replicated Data Types)

Each has its own trade-offs.

---

# 📊 Topology Comparison

| Topology | Communication | Biggest Problem | Fault Tolerance |
|-----------|---------------|-----------------|-----------------|
| Circle | Next Node | One node failure breaks chain | ⭐ |
| Star | Central Hub | Hub failure stops everything | ⭐⭐ |
| All-to-All | Everyone ↔ Everyone | Ordering & conflicts | ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐ |

